<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Well, I was raised Christian (First Christian Church, Disciples of Christ).  When I was about 13 (after I'd been baptized, so according to most southern baptists, I can't go to hell...hehe) I tried to find evidence of God in my life.  I couldn't.  I even tried talking to God.  Oddly enough, I got "We're sorry, the diety you have prayed to has been disconnected or is no longer in service."  <br>
<br>
So, I just kinda made up my mind that there was no God, and from there I started looking into the Christian beliefs to decide if that was what I really felt.  And it turns out that most of the stuff you learn in any religion is a bunch of elitist BS designed to give someone power over you.  <br>
<br>
So, once I got into High School and had thought things out for myself, and done all sorts of reasoning, I finally convinced myself that I was an atheist and the only "higher power" I belive in is, (as PO said), if there is some extraterrestrial intelligence that is more intelligent than us.  Even if there is, I'm not going to worship it.<br>
<br>
There is merit in being raised Christian.  I think that (despite evidence to the contrary on this board), I have a better moral grounding than if I hadn't grown up in that environment.  But I don't think it's necessarily the religious aspects that made me a (basically) good person, so much as being surrounded by good people so much of the time.  It was learning that I enjoyed having people be nice to me, and so I would try to be nice to them.  You can get that outside of religion with the right upbringing.<br>
